# CS509 - Assignment 2 - INDIVIDUAL (Bellman-Ford + Floyd-Warshall)


| Task Type | Algorithms | Work Mode |
|---|---|---|
| Individual task | Bellman-Ford (SSSP, negative weights allowed) and Floyd-Warshall (all-pairs), CSR graph as input | Individual |

## Assignment 2


- **Bellman-Ford** uses `read_adjacency_list()` and `build_csr()` from
  Assignment 1 unmodified. The input format (`V E`, then
  `u degree neighbor weight ...` lines, then `SOURCE s`) is exactly the
  weighted format Assignment 1 already parses - negative weights just flow
  through since edge weights are read as `double`, sign included.
- **Floyd-Warshall** is exempt from CSR per the spec (Section 4.1) and
  reads its own dense-matrix format directly - it does not touch
  Assignment 1 at all.
- The **cross-check tool** (`cross_check_driver.cpp`) builds a CSR via
  Assignment 1's `build_csr()`, then derives a dense matrix from that same
  CSR (`csr_to_dense_matrix()`) so Bellman-Ford and Floyd-Warshall are
  compared on the *exact* same graph rather than two separately-generated
  files that might drift out of sync.

## Project layout

```
include/bellman_ford.h      BellmanFordResult, bellman_ford_csr()
include/floyd_warshall.h    DenseMatrix, floyd_warshall(), read_fw_input(),
                             csr_to_dense_matrix()
src/bellman_ford.cpp        Bellman-Ford implementation (operates on Assignment 1's CSR)
src/floyd_warshall.cpp      Floyd-Warshall implementation + matrix I/O
src/bf_driver.cpp           Bellman-Ford driver (reads file, times, prints per spec)
src/fw_driver.cpp           Floyd-Warshall driver (reads file, times, prints per spec)
src/cross_check_driver.cpp  Runs BF from every source vs FW on the same graph
src/wrapper.cpp             Menu-driven runner (single test / all tests / run
                             BF+FW concurrently)
tools/gen_bf_tests.cpp      Generates bf_<V>.txt at required sizes (DAG-structured,
                             so negative weights can never form a cycle)
tools/gen_fw_tests.cpp      Generates fw_500/1000/2000.txt (same DAG trick)
tools/bf_to_fw_convert.cpp  Derives fw_10.txt / fw_100.txt from the matching
                             bf_10.txt / bf_100.txt for the required cross-check
tests/                      Spec examples + generated test files

                             
```

## Build

```bash
./build.sh # run from root 
# or, if Assignment 1 lives somewhere else: 
A1=/path/to/assignment1 ./build.sh
```

## Run

```bash
./bf_driver assignment_2/tests/bf_example.txt
./fw_driver assignment_2/tests/fw_example.txt
./cross_check assignment_2/tests/bf_gen/bf_10.txt
./wrapper          # menu-driven: single test / all tests / run both concurrently
```

## Negative-weight test-graph safety

Randomly assigning negative weights to a large graph risks accidentally
creating a negative-weight cycle, which would make most test cases report
"Negative cycle: true" instead of exercising the real algorithm. Both
generators sidestep this: every edge only goes from a lower vertex index to
a higher one (`u -> v` with `u < v`), so the generated graphs are DAGs by
construction and **cannot** contain a cycle of any kind, regardless of how
many edges are given negative weights. This lets negative and positive
weights mix freely at every size without a cycle-detection pass. Explicit
negative-cycle test cases (`bf_negative_cycle.txt`, `fw_negative_cycle.txt`)
are hand-crafted separately to verify the detection path itself works.

